 "literal": "Backwater-searched rested black-legged white heron / edge-shore screw-pine assembled great ocean / breaking wave sound-in sleeping shore-lord! / Old state loosened bangles-she here / turned-pale indeed my friend, with me too / sweet cluster mastwood-tree beautiful dappled shade / gold striped crab chased time-indeed",
 "translation": "O lord of the shore, where the black-legged white heron, having searched the backwaters, rests and sleeps amidst the screw-pine trees on the edge of the shore, lulled by the sound of the breaking waves of the great ocean! My friend, whose bangles have slipped from their old position, has indeed turned pale here, ever since the day she and I chased the gold-striped crabs in the dappled shade of the beautiful mastwood tree with sweet clusters of flowers.",
 "modern": "உப்பங்கழியில் இரையைத் தேடிவிட்டு, கரையின் ஓரத்தில் உள்ள தாழை மரங்களில் கூடி, பெரிய கடலின் கரையும் அலைகளின் ஓசையைக் கேட்டுத் தூங்கும் துறைவனே! இனிமையான பூங்கொத்துகளை உடைய புன்னை மரத்தின் புள்ளிகளுடைய நிழலில், என்னோடு சேர்ந்து பொன்னிற வரிகளையுடைய நண்டுகளைத் துரத்தி விளையாடிய அந்த நாளிலேயே, என் தோழி தன் பழைய நிலை மாறி வளையல்கள் கழலும்படி உடல் மெலிந்து இங்குப் பசலை நோய் கொண்டுவிட்டாள்.",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the heroine's friend addressing the hero who has arrived to meet the heroine. She describes his coastal land (neytal tiṇai) where a black-legged white heron, tired after hunting in the backwaters, sleeps peacefully among the screw-pine trees, undisturbed by the loud roar of the ocean waves. This image serves as an implied metaphor (ulluṟai): just as the bird sleeps peacefully despite the loud waves, the hero remains untroubled and unaware of the heroine's suffering despite her obvious physical decline. The friend points out that the heroine's bangles have become loose and she has turned pale (pasalai) ever since the days they used to play together chasing gold-striped crabs under the dappled shade of the blooming mastwood (punnai) tree. By recalling their innocent childhood play, the friend subtly reminds the hero of the long duration of their love and urges him to marry her soon to end her distress.",
